{"name":"Orange Sulphur","hex":"#e8a320","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"},{"term":"sulphur","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of sulfur."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative spelling of sulfur.","↑ “So long sulphur”, in Nature Chemistry‎, volume 1, number 5, August 2009, →DOI, →ISSN, page 333: “It is not a question of American or Oxford English spelling, it is a given name — and 'correcting' such a name to a different spelling is wholly inappropriate.”"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sulphur"}],"entries":[{"title":"Colias eurytheme","blocks":[{"text":"Colias eurytheme, the orange sulphur, also known as the alfalfa butterfly and in its larval stage as the alfalfa caterpillar, is a butterfly of the family Pieridae, where it belongs to the lowland group of \"clouded yellows and sulphurs\" subfamily Coliadinae. It is found throughout North America from southern Canada to Mexico."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/McGeorge_Colias_eurytheme.JPG/330px-McGeorge_Colias_eurytheme.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colias_eurytheme","wikidata":"Q1944775"},{"title":"Sulfur","blocks":[{"text":"Sulfur, also spelled as sulphur, is a chemical element; it has symbol S and atomic number 16. It is abundant, multivalent and nonmetallic. Under normal conditions, sulfur atoms form cyclic octatomic molecules with the chemical formula S8. Elemental sulfur is a bright yellow, crystalline solid at room temperature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Sulfur_-_El_Desierto_mine%2C_San_Pablo_de_Napa%2C_Daniel_Campos_Province%2C_Potos%C3%AD%2C_Bolivia.jpg/330px-Sulfur_-_El_Desierto_mine%2C_San_Pablo_de_Napa%2C_Daniel_Campos_Province%2C_Potos%C3%AD%2C_Bolivia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur","wikidata":"Q682"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Orange_sulphur_in_BBG_%2842933%29.jpg/500px-Orange_sulphur_in_BBG_%2842933%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Orange_sulphur_in_BBG_%2842933%29.jpg/1280px-Orange_sulphur_in_BBG_%2842933%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Orange_sulphur_in_BBG_%2842933%29.jpg/500px-Orange_sulphur_in_BBG_%2842933%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrange_sulphur_in_BBG_(42933).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or